.TH mbwcdview 1 "1 November 2026" "MB-System 5.0" "MB-System 5.0"
.SH NAME
\fBmbwcdview\fP \- Display water column data, one swath at a time.

.SH VERSION
Version 5.0

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmbwcdview\fP
.br
[
.br
\fB\-\-input\fP=\fIfile\fP {\fB\-I\fP\fIfile\fP}
.br
\fB\-\-format\fP=\fIformat_id\fP {\fB\-F\fP\fIformat_id\fP}
.br
\fB\-\-ping\fP=\fIindex\fP {\fB\-P\fP\fIindex\fP}
.br
\fB\-\-swath\fP=\fIindex\fP {\fB\-S\fP\fIindex\fP}
.br
\fB\-\-bottom\fP
.br
\fB\-\-wc-bottom\fP
.br
\fB\-\-straight\fP
.br
\fB\-\-export\fP=\fIimage.png\fP
.br
\fB\-\-size\fP=\fIwidth\fPx\fIheight\fP
.br
\fB\-\-dump\fP
.br
\fB\-\-verbose\fP {\fB\-V\fP}
.br
\fB\-\-help\fP {\fB\-H\fP}
.br
]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmbwcdview\fP is a Qt6 program that displays the water column data of swath
sonar files, read through the MBIO library, as an image of the beamformed
amplitudes against across track distance and depth, one swath at a time. Water
column data are available for the Kongsberg kmall format (format 261), in the
separate *.kmwcd files (water column only), for the third generation Kongsberg
multibeams (EM 122, 302, 710, 712, 2040 ...; formats 58 and 59), in the separate
*.wcd files or in the *.all files, and for the Reson and Teledyne 7k multibeams that
log the Compressed Water Column record, such as the T20 and T50 series (format 89),
in the *.s7k files. The original files are read; no preprocessing is needed. The
formats 56 and 57 (for instance EM 3002) are not supported.

When a Kongsberg *.kmwcd, *.kmall, *.wcd or *.all file is opened, \fBmbwcdview\fP
looks for the other file of the same survey line in the same directory: the same line number
(the first four characters of the file name) and the nearest time in the file
name. Kongsberg SIS names each file after the time of its first datagram, so the
two names may differ by seconds. The water column pings are matched with the
survey pings of the *.kmall or *.all by ping number and time. The *.kmall or *.all
supplies the navigation, the vessel heading, the transducer depth and the soundings;
the *.kmwcd or *.wcd supplies the water column. A *.all without a *.wcd supplies the
water column too, if it holds any. Any of these may be missing: without a *.kmall or
*.all the navigation is taken from the position records of the *.kmwcd or *.wcd, if
present, and depths are given below the transducer. A water column ping without a
matching survey ping is drawn at the transducer depth interpolated in time from the
survey pings around it. Where water column datagrams
were lost in a ping (*.wcd, *.all) the part of the swath they held stays blank.
A Reson 7k *.s7k file holds the survey pings and the water column together and
supplies both. Its water column beam angles are turned to the vertical with the roll
at transmit; the soundings MB-System calculates also follow the roll between transmit
and echo and the pitch, mount angles that differ between the arrays (from a platform
file) and a sound speed correction by \fBmbpreprocess\fP, and soundings logged by
Teledyne PDS include its mount angles. Where these matter the soundings lie off the
seafloor echo (see \fBmbio\fP(3)).

The water column samples are ray traced through the sound velocity profile
logged with the data (\fBmb_rt\fP(3), as \fBmbprocess\fP traces soundings), from
the transducer depth and with the beam angles and the sound speed at the
transducer logged with the water column. The soundings then lie on the seafloor
echo. Without a logged profile, or with the \fBRay trace (SVP)\fP check box off
(or \fB\-\-straight\fP), the samples are placed along straight rays with the sound
speed at the transducer;
in deep water with a fast surface layer this puts the seafloor echo noticeably
deeper than the soundings and the outer beams tens of metres off. No motion
during the ping (heave, pitch, roll) is applied.

The amplitudes are shown in dB. For Kongsberg data these are the logged values
(0.5 dB steps) minus the gain offset (TVGoffset_dB), the display value defined by
Kongsberg. In kmall data the lowest logged value (\-128, i.e. \-94 dB with a 30 dB
offset) is a fill value when the next values are practically absent. It is then drawn
as no data before the first echo of each beam (the near field), and later in the beam,
where it is a weak echo clipped to the lowest level, with the lowest colour.
In *.wcd and *.all data the amplitudes are the logged values minus the TVG
offset C, and the lowest value is a valid level, weak samples clipped to it.
In *.s7k data they are 20 log10 of the logged magnitudes, in dB relative to one
magnitude count with the sonar's gain and TVG applied; the lowest value (a magnitude
of 0 or 1) is a valid level.

The window has controls for the depth range, the amplitude range of the colour
scale, a fixed across track scale, the display of the soundings of the matching
survey ping (\fBBottom\fP: red amplitude, dark blue phase detections, filled good,
hollow flagged) and of the bottom detections recorded in the water column itself
(\fBWC bottom\fP, orange line), a slider (also mouse wheel and the left and right
arrow keys, one swath per step), a replay at a given rate, saving the image at
twice the screen resolution with the file name and ping information, and
picking positions in the image (\fBPick\fP) into a table that can be saved as text.
The image is drawn on the graphics card through Qt's QRhi (Metal on macOS,
Direct3D 11 on Windows, OpenGL on Linux), or with OpenGL where \fBmbwcdview\fP
was built with Qt older than 6.8, so stepping through the pings is fast.

The depth range starts at the surface, or, for a sonar deeper than its longest
slant range (on a vehicle), a tenth of that range above the shallowest transducer
depth; so does the image written with \fB\-\-export\fP.

Without a window, \fB\-\-dump\fP prints a summary of a survey line and
\fB\-\-export\fP writes an image of one swath.

